From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: describe the DisplayPort transmitter

The device-tree side of `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`. One node, and it describes a block rather than a
display pipeline: there is no TCON, no display engine and no output port graph
in this tree yet, so none of that is invented here.

Identity is not assumed. With the block ungated by hand on the 8 GiB board, CORE_ID at
0x057400FC reads 0x000A0509 -- the value UM 15.4.7 documents for this core -- and
the AUX channel behind it returned DPCD and a checksum-valid EDID. So the compatible names a
block that has answered on this silicon.

reg
---

`<0x0 0x05740000 0x0 0x1000>`, the control window (and the head
of the vendor's own capture window).

⚠ **The vendor also maps eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000 and this node deliberately
does not.** Its clock gates (`LNK_CLK_GATING`, `VID0/VID1`) read 0x00000000
through an entire successful AUX session including a full 128-byte EDID fetch --
measured, not assumed -- so nothing on the AUX path needs them. The window goes
in when a patch is shown to need it, which is most likely the one that first
drives pixels.

interrupts
----------

INTID 94 "EDP" = **GIC_SPI 62** (A733 user manual). The driver polls AUX_STATUS's latched companion and does not
request this interrupt yet. It is here because the node's job is to describe the
hardware, and because link training will want it -- not because anything reads
it today. That is stated so nobody later reads its presence as a claim.

clocks and resets
-----------------

`CLK_BUS_EDP` and `RST_BUS_EDP` from `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`. **Both are required**, and that is a
measurement rather than a convention: with the gate set and the reset still
asserted the entire 4 KiB window reads 0x00000000; only with EDP_RST de-asserted
does CORE_ID answer. A node that carried the clock alone would build, probe, and
read zeros.

No `assigned-clock-rates`: the AUX divider is computed from the bus clock at
runtime rather than the clock being bent to fit a hardcoded divider.

phys
----

`<&aux_hpd>` only, `phy-names = "aux-phy"`. The AUX differential pair runs to the
Type-C SBU pins through its own analog front end (`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy` and `arm64-dts-a733-aux-hpd-phy-node`), and COMB0 -- which
carries the four main-link lanes -- is not on this path at all: the by-hand run
completed a full EDID fetch with COMB0 untouched by this block. The vendor's
shape is `phys = <&combo0_dp>, <&aux_hpd_phy>`, and reproducing it would mean
giving the HW-proven USB3 combo-PHY node a second phy cell for no present
consumer. The main link joins when something drives it.

no supplies
-----------

The vendor's `vdd-edp` / `vcc-edp` are panel rails, enabled from its panel path;
this board has no panel, so there is nothing to name. The rail that does feed
this path is AVDD-HPD-AUX and it belongs to the AUX/HPD node, where it is
recorded as unnamed rather than filled with a `regulator-fixed` stand-in.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 on the full series, the dtb builds with no new dtc
warning, and the compiled dtb carries displayport@5740000 with its compatible,
reg, interrupt, clock, reset and phy. NOT verified: the board.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1300,6 +1300,55 @@
 		 * The old note here said "IRQ 185" and mixed the two numbering
 		 * spaces: INTID 185 is PCIE0_MSI.
 		 */
+		/*
+		 * The DisplayPort transmitter -- a Trilinear DP 1.4 core, identified
+		 * by its own CORE_ID register reading 0x000A0509, the value UM
+		 * 15.4.7 documents (measured on the 8 GiB board 2026-08-18 after ungating the
+		 * block by hand).
+		 *
+		 * Only the AUX channel is driven today, so this node describes the
+		 * block and not yet a display pipeline: no TCON, no display engine,
+		 * no output port graph. Those arrive with the KMS work and extend
+		 * this node rather than replacing it.
+		 *
+		 * reg: the 4 KiB control window. The second window the vendor also
+		 * maps -- eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000 -- is deliberately NOT here: its
+		 * clock gates read 0x00000000 through an entire successful AUX
+		 * session including a full EDID fetch, so nothing on this path needs
+		 * it. It goes in when something is shown to need it.
+		 *
+		 * interrupts: INTID 94 "EDP" = GIC_SPI 62 (interrupt list of the
+		 * A733 user manual). The AUX driver polls AUX_STATUS and does not
+		 * request this yet; it is here because it describes the hardware,
+		 * which is this file's job, and because link training will want it.
+		 *
+		 * clocks/resets: the eDP bus gate and reset, EDP_BGR_REG bit 0 and
+		 * bit 16. BOTH are required before a single register of the block is
+		 * reachable -- with the gate alone the whole window reads zero,
+		 * measured, so the reset is not a formality here.
+		 *
+		 * phys: the AUX differential pair runs to the Type-C SBU pins through
+		 * its own analog front end (`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy` and `arm64-dts-a733-aux-hpd-phy-node`), not through COMB0. COMB0
+		 * carries the four main-link lanes and is reached through the Type-C
+		 * mux instead, so it is deliberately absent from this list.
+		 *
+		 * No supplies. The vendor's `vdd-edp`/`vcc-edp` are panel rails and
+		 * this board has no panel; the rail that does feed this path is
+		 * AVDD-HPD-AUX and it belongs to the AUX/HPD node. A regulator-fixed
+		 * stand-in would satisfy the binding and prove nothing.
+		 */
+		edp0: displayport@5740000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dp";
+			reg = <0x0 0x05740000 0x0 0x1000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 62 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_EDP>;
+			clock-names = "bus";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_EDP>;
+			phys = <&aux_hpd>;
+			phy-names = "aux-phy";
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
 		usb3: usb@6a00000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3";
 			reg = <0x0 0x06a00000 0x0 0x10000>;
